WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.460
How did two small animals from opposite ends of the world upset the reputations of leading

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:12.640
scientists and change our understanding of evolution?

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:18.000
I've been lucky enough one way or another to meet some of our planet's most enchanting

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.120
creatures, but some I find particularly intriguing.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:28.700
We've known about some of these creatures for centuries.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:32.480
Others we have discovered more recently.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:46.200
In this series, I share their stories and reveal why they really are natural curiosities.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.160
In this program, we explore the stories of two animals that sent shockwaves through the

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:54.840
scientific world and beyond.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:01:00.160
One is a toad that became the center of a scientific storm and caused accusations of

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:05.600
fakery in the early part of the 20th century.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:10.760
The other is an Australian animal that baffled the greatest thinkers of Victorian Europe

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:27.480
and caused many to question whether it was even real.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:32.080
When the first Europeans arrived in Australia, they were shocked by the animals they found

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:33.480
there.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:39.360
Nothing in Europe could compare with the bizarre, upright grazers hopping across the grassland

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.200
landscape carrying their young in pouches.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.400
Kangaroos were obvious oddities, but another even stranger creature also caught the attention

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.000
of early settlers.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.760
It lived along riverbanks and swam in the water.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:02:03.240
Those first Europeans who saw it called it a water mole, but that name didn't last long.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:16.600
Inside this box is one of the first specimens of platypus ever to be seen outside Australia.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:23.640
It was sent to England in 1798 by Captain John Hunter, the governor of New South Wales.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:29.480
This one small animal would take the scientific world by storm and transform the careers and

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:34.960
reputations of some of the leading thinkers of the time.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:40.880
The platypus seemed to be a concoction of different animals, part bird with its bill

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:44.520
and part mammal with its furry body.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:48.920
When Charles Darwin first encountered one in the wild, it baffled even him.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:56.600
Surely he wrote, two distinct creators must have been at work.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:02.360
The task of describing the first platypus specimen fell to naturalist George Shaw, who

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:06.440
worked in the Department of Natural History in the British Museum.

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:12.640
And he viewed this remarkable specimen with a fair degree of caution.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:17.280
This is a first edition of a journal called Naturalist Miscellany, which was published

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.440
a few years after his examination.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:26.280
And it contains not only an article by him, but a nice picture of the animal concerned.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:33.680
And at the end he says, on a subject so extraordinary as the present, a degree of skepticism is

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.880
not only pardonable, but laudable.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:41.520
And I ought perhaps to acknowledge that I almost doubt the testimony of my own eyes

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.960
with respect to the structure of this animal's beak.

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:50.360
It's said that Shaw was so determined to make sure that he was not a victim of some elaborate

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:55.560
hoax that he actually cut behind the bill to make sure it hadn't been sewn on by some

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:58.720
mischievous forger.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:02.320
In the late 18th century, the world was opening up.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:06.360
Travellers were returning from overseas with all kinds of wonders.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:10.880
Among them were specimens of creatures that people had come to think of as being myths,

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:13.800
such as merman and mermaids.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:18.400
These were, of course, hoaxes put together with parts from different animals.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:26.600
So it's understandable that Shaw had doubts about the authenticity of his new furry specimen.

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:32.600
Despite his misgivings, he decided to give it a scientific name, platypus, which means

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:34.200
flat-footed.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:38.840
He didn't know, however, that a beetle had already been given this name, and some years

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:45.040
later another taxonomist very properly gave it a new one, ornithorhynchus, which means

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:46.560
bird snout.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:51.920
But platypus is still the name that most people use.

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:54.200
But what type of creature was it?

00:04:54.200 --> 00:05:00.640
George Shaw believed it to be a mammal because of its furry body.

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:05.960
All mammals feed on milk during the first part of their lives, milk that is produced

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:09.320
by their mother's mammary glands.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:13.120
But could an animal with a large flat bill really suckle?

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:18.080
Some scientists thought that was impossible, and anyway they couldn't believe the platypus

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:21.680
and the monkey could belong to the same group of animals.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:25.320
But that view was to change.

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:30.520
Some 30 years after George Shaw described the platypus, a German naturalist, Johann

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:35.960
Mechel, produced this wonderful collection of anatomical studies.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:42.200
Mechel's meticulous and detailed work would help identify the true nature of this animal.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:51.920
Here we can see his drawing of a male platypus showing clearly the claw.

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:56.160
Mechel also reported the existence of simple glands beneath the thick fur of the female

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:00.160
platypus, glands that he suggested secreted milk.

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:05.960
There could be little doubt that these glands produced something, but even then several

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:11.360
scientists doubted Mechel's claims and suggested rather desperately that the glands secreted

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:14.080
not milk, but a lubricant.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:19.880
Today we know that Mechel was right, and I was once able to use an optical probe to peer

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:25.440
into a platypus' burrow and see a female platypus nurturing her single baby.

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:29.080
Yes, and there it is.

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:31.240
It's milk.

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:33.240
Milk is the perfect food.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:37.680
It provides the growing youngster with everything it wants.

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:41.280
And only mammals produce milk.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:44.520
In most mammals, of course, it comes from an apple.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:50.920
But in this very primitive mammal, it simply oozes through the skin.

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:55.680
But 19th century biologists had no such tricks to help them.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:00.960
They had to unravel the strange biology of Australian mammals from just a few shriveled

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:05.200
remains of long-dead specimens.

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:09.960
Forty years after their discovery of the platypus, a brilliant young anatomist who was to become

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:14.480
a giant of 19th century science joined the debate.

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:16.960
This is a statue of Richard Owen.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:22.680
Owen was a formidable man, the founding director of the Natural History Museum in Britain.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:28.480
He was once described as having so much brain as to require two hats.

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:32.680
The platypus would become a central character in Owen's career.

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:36.960
His work on this small creature would help him secure election to the prestigious Royal

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:42.760
Society, an exclusive group of scientists and thinkers.

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:47.800
Owen had an advantage over his European colleagues.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:56.480
Australia was a British colony, and Owen used his contacts to supply him with specimens.

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:01.600
Eventually two baby platypuses arrived, and it was obvious to him that they would have

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:05.280
no difficulty in suckling.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:09.480
Owen had not yet developed the bill that would have made it awkward.

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:14.760
So he accepted that platypus babies, like other mammal babies, were indeed raised on

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:18.400
milk.

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:23.200
But the biggest mystery of the platypus was still unsolved.

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:30.960
Did this animal lay eggs, just like reptiles or birds, or did it give birth to live young?

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:34.440
Owen was at the heart of that debate.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:39.000
These jars contained the bodies of several platypus that were shot and sent back here

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:42.400
to the museum for Richard Owen to examine.

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:46.480
His determination to prove whether or not they laid eggs was going to cause the death

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:50.000
of quite a number of platypus.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:55.080
The Australian aborigines were absolutely clear they did lay eggs, but that was not

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:56.380
good enough for Owen.

00:08:56.380 --> 00:09:00.360
He knew better than any Australian aboriginal.

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:05.000
He did concede that it might be that the eggs were retained inside the body and hatched

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:10.280
there so that the young were born live, but that's as far as he'd go.

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:12.400
Eggs were also sent back.

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:16.360
Some of them were fake, and some of them belonged to snakes.

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:23.560
It was going to be some decades before the puzzle of the platypus was finally solved.

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:30.240
The platypus now became embroiled in the greatest scientific debate of the Victorian era.

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:33.800
Did species evolve or were they created?

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:39.680
Darwin's theory of evolution suggested that species could change over time, so an intermediate

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:44.640
form that laid eggs but had fur like a mammal was to be expected.

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:49.920
But that was too much of a stretch even for Owen's great brain.

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:55.840
In 1884, more than 80 years after this first platypus specimen had been examined by George

00:09:55.840 --> 00:10:03.280
Shaw, William Hay Caldwell arrived in Australia funded by a Royal Society scholarship.

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:08.100
One of his main aims was to solve the platypus egg question once and for all.

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:12.400
After several months in Queensland and with the help of the local aborigines, he finally

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:13.960
got the answer.

00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:20.320
He shot a female platypus soon after she had laid an egg in her nest burrow with the second

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:26.300
egg about to emerge from her vent and they looked like this.

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:32.340
It was at last visible evidence that this animal did indeed lay eggs.

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:35.240
He sent a telegram to a scientific gathering in Montreal.

00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:42.800
It was brief and to the point, monotremes oviparous ovum meroblastic.

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:48.760
These four words, to the scientifically initiated, meant that the platypus laid eggs and that

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:54.280
the eggs consisted of an undivided large yolk just like a bird's egg.

00:10:54.280 --> 00:11:00.280
The mystery was at last solved.

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:05.440
Richard Owen, who had refused to believe a mammal could lay an egg, was by now 80 years

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:11.420
old and he was no longer held in the same esteem as in the early part of his career.

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:17.600
The platypus had helped establish his reputation but now the riddle of this creature's reproduction

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:20.920
had proved him wrong.

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:25.520
It's extraordinary to think that this small animal fooled and confounded many of the great

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:28.280
scientific minds of 19th century Europe.

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:34.960
Not a hoax but a true curiosity and one like no other.

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:40.640
Natural curiosities will continue with another tale of forgery as we unravel the mystery

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:42.560
of the midwife toad.

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:49.000
The egg laying platypus was hardly believable to Victorian researchers but evolution has

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:54.880
thrown up many unusual mating strategies and in the early part of the 20th century, the

00:11:54.880 --> 00:12:00.480
anatomy of a particular amphibian started an argument that, like the platypus, led to

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:03.280
accusations of forgery.

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:08.360
This is the curious tale of the midwife toad.

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:13.040
Mif toads are not native to Britain, they were introduced about a century ago and since

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:16.240
then have been slowly spreading over England.

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:23.720
Their natural home is Europe, from Germany to Spain and in the 1920s their mating habits

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:28.920
caused a media sensation.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:34.080
Investigations into the way the body of the male toad changed according to its environment

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:40.280
led some to believe it might be possible to breed a race of super humans.

00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:45.720
To understand why, we must first know what makes the midwife toad so different from any

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:49.280
other frog or toad.

00:12:49.280 --> 00:12:53.480
Amphibians were among the first backbone animals to take to the land.

00:12:53.480 --> 00:13:01.040
Since then, they've colonized most habitats, from rainforests to deserts and mountains.

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:06.080
Despite spending much of their lives on land, most frogs and toads need water to reproduce,

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:10.040
whether it be in a small vase plant or a large lake.

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:13.760
But mating in water is a slippery business.

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:16.880
Male toads, however, have a special adaptation.

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:22.180
Black warty swellings on their wrists called nuptial pads, which enable them to grip their

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:24.920
partners securely during sex.

00:13:24.920 --> 00:13:30.400
Once the female produces her eggs, the male releases his sperm and then lets go.

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:32.160
This job is done.

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:34.520
But midwife toads are different.

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:39.360
The male does not have nuptial pads on his wrists.

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:42.400
And that's because he doesn't mate in water.

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:44.000
He mates on land.

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:51.320
The female produces her eggs and then he takes them around his legs with an action that's

00:13:51.320 --> 00:13:58.200
been compared to a man trying to put on his trousers without using his hands.

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:04.480
So it is the male toad that is the actual midwife, not the female.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:10.160
Midwife toads tend to live in places where open water is scarce.

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:15.280
Once the male has successfully wrapped a string of eggs around his legs, he usually hides

00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:23.880
under a rock where it's suitably damp.

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:31.280
He may have as many as 150 eggs and he hides away for up to two months while they develop.

00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:39.880
Then just before the eggs hatch, he sets off to find water for his emerging tadpoles.

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:47.280
Now the tadpoles of most frogs and toads turn into the adult form within a matter of weeks.

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:49.220
But not so the midwife toad.

00:14:49.220 --> 00:14:51.820
It takes much, much longer.

00:14:51.820 --> 00:14:58.760
In fact, sometimes they may even overwinter in the form of a tadpole, which is why perhaps

00:14:58.760 --> 00:15:04.160
midwife toad tadpoles are such whoppers.

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:09.200
Frogs and toads are widely used in biological studies because they're easy to keep and

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:13.680
the different stages of their life cycles are easy to observe.

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:18.640
So it's no surprise that the unusual behavior of the midwife toad should attract the attention

00:15:18.640 --> 00:15:21.640
of many biologists.

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:26.280
One was an Austrian scientist called Paul Kammerer, who worked in Vienna in the early

00:15:26.280 --> 00:15:28.960
part of the 20th century.

00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:33.840
And his discoveries quickly brought him great fame.

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:41.480
But the toad would become a curse that would haunt him until the end of his life.

00:15:41.480 --> 00:15:47.280
Kammerer was greatly influenced by the great French zoologist Jean-Baptiste Lamarck, who

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:54.400
in 1799 published his theory that characteristics acquired by an animal during its life could

00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:56.960
be inherited by its offspring.

00:15:56.960 --> 00:16:02.480
That a giraffe, for example, reaching upwards to nibble the topmost shoots of trees would

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:08.120
over time lengthen its neck muscles and that this increase would then be inherited by its

00:16:08.120 --> 00:16:12.680
offspring and so on for generation after generation.

00:16:12.680 --> 00:16:19.280
Lamarck's theory was largely rejected after Charles Darwin proposed a different mechanism

00:16:19.280 --> 00:16:25.560
for evolution based on changes to an animal's genetic makeup.

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:29.840
Kammerer was keen to prove that Lamarck was right after all.

00:16:29.840 --> 00:16:34.480
But giraffes are not the ideal experimental animal, so he needed one he could keep in

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:41.280
a lab and that would reproduce quickly and his attention fell on the midwife toad.

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:46.960
Kammerer became fascinated with the unusual nature of the midwife toads reproduction.

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:53.840
Why did males, like this one, carry eggs around his legs and could this be changed?

00:16:53.840 --> 00:16:58.840
He wondered if their biology might be related to their natural environment, which is largely

00:16:58.840 --> 00:17:01.000
arid.

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:05.400
Kammerer decided to see what would happen if he kept the toads in a warm, humid tank

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:08.800
with access to pools of cool water.

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:14.080
His work with the toads would last many years and involve several generations, but eventually

00:17:14.080 --> 00:17:16.480
he noticed changes.

00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:22.720
Some male toads abandoned carrying the eggs and instead the females laid them directly

00:17:22.720 --> 00:17:25.960
in water.

00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:32.460
Over several generations, Kammerer had managed to change the midwife toad from being a land-breeding

00:17:32.460 --> 00:17:36.600
animal to one that bred in water.

00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:41.840
But the most extraordinary discovery came as he continued breeding these toads.

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:47.240
He noticed that the wrists of some of the males developed warty-looking structures,

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:52.580
just like the nuptial pads of other frogs and toads, which are normally used by males

00:17:52.580 --> 00:17:56.720
to grip females when fertilising her eggs.

00:17:56.720 --> 00:18:02.080
His work suggested that somehow, by altering the environment in which they lived, a toad's

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:08.880
body could be changed, and that change was then passed on to future generations.

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:14.320
Kammerer's work was taking place at the end of the First World War, and political movements

00:18:14.320 --> 00:18:19.680
on the left and the right were then keen to exploit scientific discoveries.

00:18:19.680 --> 00:18:25.560
Despite his subject being a small toad, some saw an opportunity to extend his findings

00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:28.480
beyond the laboratory.

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:32.760
He was hailed as a second Darwin in the New York Times.

00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:37.080
Some newspapers got carried away and suggested that Kammerer's discoveries could apply to

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:38.300
humans.

00:18:38.300 --> 00:18:43.520
His work could help, in other words, to breed a race of superhumans.

00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:48.300
Whether he liked it or not, Kammerer was now in the spotlight.

00:18:48.300 --> 00:18:51.800
He set off on a lecture tour across Europe and America.

00:18:51.800 --> 00:18:56.360
In Cambridge, the professor of zoology hailed his achievements and put one of Kammerer's

00:18:56.360 --> 00:18:59.320
toads on display.

00:18:59.320 --> 00:19:01.520
But not everyone was convinced.

00:19:01.520 --> 00:19:04.120
An American zoologist by the name of G.K.

00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:09.960
Noble wrote a damning article in the prestigious scientific journal Nature.

00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:14.240
Noble examined one of Kammerer's toads and declared that its black nuptial pads were

00:19:14.240 --> 00:19:19.420
fakes produced by injecting a black dye.

00:19:19.420 --> 00:19:20.420
Kammerer denied this.

00:19:20.420 --> 00:19:25.840
Someone, he said, had interfered with his specimens and was trying to ruin him.

00:19:25.840 --> 00:19:29.240
But the damage to his name was done.

00:19:29.240 --> 00:19:34.200
Six weeks after the Nature article accusing him of forgery, Kammerer wrote a letter to

00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:36.920
another leading scientific journal.

00:19:36.920 --> 00:19:40.280
This is an extract of what it said.

00:19:40.280 --> 00:19:47.000
On the basis of this state of affairs, I dare not, although I myself have had no part in

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:53.640
these falsifications of my prior specimens, any longer consider myself a proper man to

00:19:53.640 --> 00:19:55.960
accept your call.

00:19:55.960 --> 00:20:01.160
I see that I am also not in a position to endure this wrecking of my life's work, and

00:20:01.160 --> 00:20:06.360
I hope I shall gather together enough courage and strength to put an end to my wrecked life

00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:09.400
tomorrow.

00:20:09.400 --> 00:20:16.600
Soon after writing that letter, he walked into the hills around his home and shot himself.

00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:20.920
Whether or not Kammerer's suicide was purely down to the fallout from his midwife toad

00:20:20.920 --> 00:20:23.640
experiments, we can't be sure.

00:20:23.640 --> 00:20:28.280
There were many other problems in his personal life, but there can be little doubt that the

00:20:28.280 --> 00:20:34.320
scandal surrounding his work would have weighed heavily on his mind.

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:40.320
Since Kammerer's death, a specimen of male midwife toad with nopcial paths has been found

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:42.080
in the wild.

00:20:42.080 --> 00:20:48.640
Some scientists now believe that environmental influences can change the way some genes behave,

00:20:48.640 --> 00:20:53.920
and that these changes can indeed be passed on to the next generation.

00:20:53.920 --> 00:20:59.420
Perhaps midwife toads possessed the gene to grow these structures, but it's only switched

00:20:59.420 --> 00:21:02.180
on in certain situations.

00:21:02.180 --> 00:21:05.160
Does this prove Kammerer was right?

00:21:05.160 --> 00:21:09.560
No one has been able to repeat Kammerer's experiments with midwife toads, so we don't

00:21:09.560 --> 00:21:13.960
know for sure if he falsified his findings or whether he had stumbled upon a quirk of

00:21:13.960 --> 00:21:20.160
inheritance ahead of its time and beyond the understanding of scientists of his era.

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:25.560
What is certain is that the nature of how species inherit their characteristics is more

00:21:25.560 --> 00:21:32.160
complex than he or others at the time originally thought.

00:21:32.160 --> 00:21:37.760
The curious lives of the midwife toad on the Dugbyl patipus perplexed and wrong-footed

00:21:37.760 --> 00:21:44.160
science for some considerable time, but in the end, both these creatures helped us to

00:21:44.160 --> 00:22:10.640
better understand the way animals evolve.

00:22:14.160 --> 00:22:15.160
.
